  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1011 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nMid-level high now over east Texas, with upper-level high gradually \ndrifting east to join. Monday begins a 2-3 day ramp up in \nthunderstorm coverage as upper high continues to drift east as far \nas the southern Appalachians by Tuesday. Models still showing \nridging back west over the CWA for Monday and Tuesday, perhaps \nconcentrating best moisture/chance of rain over the western half of \nthe CWA. \n\nWednesday into the weekend...upper high begins drifting back west \nbecoming more concentric, allowing better southerly flow aloft to \nspread across our area. This should distribute the moisture and rain \nchances more evenly across the entire forecast area. Overall the \nmodels have been pretty consistent Monday into the weekend.\nThe highest PWs arrive Thursday and Friday. Thus it still looks like \nbest area-wide storm coverage and risk of flash flooding is on these \ntwo days. Models still showing numerous weak short waves moving \nnorth across the area Wednesday and beyond. A couple of these waves \nphase in with the mid-latitude jet Thursday and Friday, sweeping \nthem east both days. \n\nModels do begin diverging on their solutions after Friday. GFS shows \nmain mid-latitude trough Saturday evening, and flushing the moisture \neast of the area Sunday and moving a fair weather ridge into the \narea Monday. ECMWF slower with this trough, not moving through and \nflushing the moisture until Monday. \n\nTemperatures will begin a downward trend this week--we may have seen \nour last chance today for triple digits. By Thursday into the \nweekend we may even see below normal high temperatures.",
